# "The Brien Farm and Family" (Marcella Sherfy, Resources Management Division, Gettysburg National Military Park, June 1972): second reading, workstream bt-web, 2026-11-15.
# The first reading took five passages from PDF pp. 6-9. This reading takes the house of 1863 and its remodeling (pp. 3-4), the farm of July 1863 and the removal of its fences (p. 5), the damage claims (pp. 10-11), the appendix on the farm layout (pp. 15-18: maps, fences, the second house on the Emmitsburg Road, the lane, house and barn) and the farmyard fencing plan (pp. 23-25).
# The text layer is a typescript OCR. Every quote is typed in cleaned form (letters and digits must be those of one passage of the page; the damaged letters are listed as {{saved|fixed}} pairs). Pages 4, 11, 15, 16, 17, 23 and 25 were looked at as page images and every digit was read from the image: the typewriter 4 of this scan reads as 5 in the text layer (the page image of p. 15 shows 194 panels and $174, the text layer says 195 and 175: the quote carries the pair).
# Maps named by the study (Brien's own drawing for his damage claims, the Warren map of 1868-69, a tract map of 1895, the Cope map of 1896) and the Brady photograph (a photograph the study dates to shortly after the battle) are the study's own evidence; their state is carried in the state date of each entry. A map made after July 1863 is never an 1863 profile.
@ NPS-BRIEN-FARM
%basis Brien's damage claims (1870s), the Warren map (1868-69), the Cope map (1896) and the Brady photograph, as the study names them
%images 4, 11, 15, 16, 17, 23, 25

3 | buildings | Brien (Bryan) house in 1863: a small story and a half structure | bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-HOUSE1 | 1863 (before the remodeling of the next entry) | stated | The study says the Briens' small story and a half house, which the park knows by its wartime appearance, was later remodeled into a two-story L-shaped house sometime between 1863 and 1880 (the remodeled form is carried in the next entry) | We know that their small story and a half structure was remodeled into a | @4: two story, L shaped house sometime between 1863 and 1880
4 | buildings | Brien house remodeled into a two-story L-shaped house sometime between 1863 and 1880 | bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-HOUSE1 | !1863-1880 (remodeling after the battle; not in the 1863 scene) | stated | The study says the small story and a half structure of 1863 was remodeled into a two-story L-shaped house sometime between 1863 and 1880 | two story, L shaped house sometime between 1863 and 1880 | @3: We know that their small story and a half structure was remodeled into a
4 | buildings | How the house looked in 1863 is known for two sides only, and the date of the remodeling is uncertain | bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-HOUSE1 | 1863 (gap stated by the study) | stated | The study says that only two sides of the house are known as they appeared in 1863 and that it is uncertain when and exactly how the house was remodeled | =We know, for instance, how only two sides of the house appeared in 1863. We are uncertain of when and precisely how the house was remodeled.
4 | buildings | The 1863 photograph does not let the study tell battle damage from structure | bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-HOUSE1 | 1863 (gap stated by the study) | stated | The study says that in the 1863 photograph it has not been able to determine which portions show battle damage and which are structural clues | =In the 1863 photograph, we have not been able to determine which portions show battle damage and which are structural clues.
5 | fields | Brien farm on July 1, 1863: a small farm raising the common crops, with two houses and a set of farm buildings | bld-brian-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The study says that on July 1, 1863 Abraham Brien owned a small farm on the southern outskirts of Gettysburg on which he raised the locale's common crops and maintained two houses and a set of farm buildings | =On July 1, 1863, Abraham Brien owned a small farm on the southern outskirts of Gettysburg. On it, he raised the locale's common crops and maintained two houses and a set of farm buildings.
5 | fences | Union soldiers removed Brien's fences for firewood and fortifications within three days of July 1, 1863 | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The study says that within the next three days after July 1, 1863 Union soldiers removed Brien's fences for firewood and fortifications and ate his garden vegetables before the Confederate soldiers of Pickett's Charge met them on his land | =Within the next three days, Union soldiers removed his fences for firewood and fortifications, ate his garden vegetables, and met the Confederate soldiers of "Pickett's Charge" on his land. | On July 1, 1863, Abraham Brien owned a small farm on the southern outskirts of Gettysburg.
10 | damage | Brien's claims itemize stones for stone walls, panels of post fence, hay and crop damage; only general values for the houses and farm buildings | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm | 1863-07 (claims filed in the 1870s) | stated | The study says Brien's damage claims itemized the number of stones needed to replace stone walls, the number of panels of post fence destroyed and a specific amount of hay and crop damage, but gave only general values for the damage done to the houses and farm buildings | #s3-4
11 | general | Brien's own claim map reverses the positions of house and barn against the regimental histories, the Warren map and the Tipton photographs, and claims 20 acres against the 12 of the tax collectors | bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-HOUSE1,STR-BRYAN-BARN1 | 1870s (Brien's claim map) against 1869 (Warren map) and the late 1800s (Tipton photographs) | conflict | The study says that on Brien's claim map the position of his house and barn is reversed from that described in regimental histories, shown on the Warren map of 1869 and indicated in W. H. Tipton photographs of the late 1800s, and that he claims 20 acres of land instead of the 12 indicated by tax collectors, so that very little certain knowledge can be based upon the claims | =Yet, the position of Brien's house and barn are reversed from that described in regimental histories, shown on the G. K. Warren Map of 1869 and indicated in W. H. Tipton photographs of the late 1800's. And there, as in his prose statement, he claims 20 acres of land instead of the 12 indicated by tax collectors.
15 | general | The four maps of the Brien farm: Brien's claim drawing, Warren's 1868-1869 map, a tract map of about 1895 and Cope's map of 1896 | bld-brian-farm,FEN-brian | ~1868-1896 (maps made after the battle) | stated | The study says that, lacking a deed description, the layout of the Brien farm must be read from four maps in chronological order: Brien's drawing for his damage claims, General Warren's 1868-1869 battlefield map, a tract map of about the time of the 1895 purchase and Lt. Colonel Cope's map for the 1896 report of the park commission | #s1-2
15 | fences | Warren and Cope disagree on the Brien fences: Virginia worm fence or post and rail along the Emmitsburg Road; post and rail or stone north of the buildings; Virginia worm fence or stone wall south of the house | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) against 1896 (Cope map): the 1863 types are not resolved | conflict | The study says the Warren map shows a Virginia worm fence along the Emmitsburg Road where the Cope map shows a post and rail, a post and rail fence on the north side of the buildings where Cope shows a stone fence beginning west of the barn, and a Virginia worm fence along a field south of the house where Cope continues the stone wall | #s5-6
15 | fences | Brien's damage claims indicate a preponderance of wooden fencing: 194 panels of post-and-rail and worm fence worth $174 and stone fence destroyed worth $5.00 | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm | 1863-07 (claims of the 1870s) | stated | The study says that no method resolves the differences between the maps, but that Brien's damage claims indicate a preponderance of wooden fencing, listing 194 panels of fence (part post and rail and part worm) worth $174 and stone fence destroyed worth $5.00 | =No positive method exists to correctly resolve these differences, but Brien's damage claims indicate a preponderance of wooden fencing on his property. {{(195|(194}} panels of fence--part post and rail and part worm - worth {{175;|$174;}} stone fence destroyed worth $5.00).
16 | fences | The fence lines of the Warren and Cope maps are virtually identical despite different materials, and do not follow the claim map or the 1895 tract map boundaries | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm | ~1868-1896 (Warren and Cope maps; the 1895 tract map) | stated | The study says the fence lines on the Warren and Cope maps are virtually identical in line despite differences in fencing materials, that they do not follow the boundary lines on Brien's damage claims or on the 1895 tract map, and that no way exists to identify the Brien farm boundaries with certainty | =Fencelines on both the Warren and Cope maps are virtually identical, despite differences in fencing materials. These fence lines, however, do not follow the boundary lines {{shorn|shown}} on Brien's damage claims or those on the tract map of property purchased from the heirs of Jacob Benner in 1895. Again, {{vxith|with}} no deed description or other reliable source, no way {{oxists|exists}} to positively identify Brien farm boundaries.
16 | general | The Taneytown Road tract was evidently sold separately between 1868 and 1895 | bld-brian-farm | 1868-1895 (sale of the Taneytown Road tract, hedged) | hedged | The study says the Taneytown Road tract of the Brien land was evidently sold separately between 1868 and 1895, so the park did not buy all of the Brien land from the Benners in 1895 | =The Taneytown Road tract was evidently sold separately between 1868 and 1895.
16 | fences | The Warren map has no fences near the western part of the Brien property, which suggests his boundaries followed the fences (a judgment) | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) | hedged | The study says the lack of adjacent fence lines in the western area of Brien's property on the Warren map encourages the belief that his boundaries conform to the fences, and calls this a judgment | =Lack of adjacent fence lines in the western area of Brien's property on the Warren map encourage the belief that Brien's boundaries conform to the fences. This is, however, a judgment.
16 | buildings | Brien's second house on the Emmitsburg Road: a small story and a half structure whose exact location has never been established | bld-brian-farm | 1863 | stated | The study says Brien owned a small story and a half structure on the Emmitsburg Road whose exact location has never been established | Brien owned a small story and a half structure on the Emmitsburg Road, but its exact location has never been established.
16 | buildings | Position of the second house: north of Brien's own house on the claim map and the Warren map, directly west of it on the Cope map and in the Brady photograph | bld-brian-farm | 1863 against ~1868-1896 maps (the sources disagree) | conflict | The study says Brien's damage claim map and the Warren map put the Emmitsburg Road structure north of Brien's own house while the Cope map and the Brady photograph appear to show it directly west of Brien's own | =Brien's damage claim map and the Warren map indicate that this structure was north of Brien's own house. The Cope map and the Brady photograph, however, appear to show that house directly west of Brien's own.
17 | general | No map shows the lane from the Emmitsburg Road to Brien's house; Bicknell put it close to the house and Shields a large oak at its head | bld-brian-farm | 1863-07 (soldiers' accounts) | stated | The study says none of the maps shows the lane that ran from the Emmitsburg Road to Brien's house, that Lt. L. E. Bicknell of the First Company Massachusetts Sharpshooters reported the lane close to the house and that Captain David Shields observed a large oak at its head | =None of the maps available show the lane which ran from the Emmitsburg Road to Brien's own house. Lt. L. E. Bicknell, First Company Massachusetts Sharpshooters, reported in a battle summary that the Brien lane was close to the house; and Captain David Shields observed that a large Oak stood at the head of the lane.
17 | general | In Brady's photograph the oak stands a little north and west of the house; the lane possibly followed the fence line to the Emmitsburg Road | bld-brian-farm,FEN-brian | 1863 (photograph shortly after the battle); the lane line is the study's inference | hedged | The study says the oak appears in Brady's photograph a little north and west of the house and that the lane possibly followed the fence line to the Emmitsburg Road | =In Brady's photograph, the Oak appears a little north and west of the house. Possibly then, the lane followed the fence line to the Emmitsburg Road.
17 | buildings | House and barn: all maps put the house north and east of the barn; Warren 30 feet apart, Cope 81 feet | bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-HOUSE1,STR-BRYAN-BARN1 | ~1868-1896 (Warren map and Cope map disagree) | conflict | The study says all the maps put the house north and east of the barn, with Warren placing the distance between the two at 30 feet and Cope at 81 feet | =All indicate that the house is north and east of the {{b a m|barn,}} Warren placed the distance between the two at 30 feet, while Cope placed it at 81 feet.
18 | general | The park has consistently used the Warren map, partly because the Brady photograph agrees with it around the house | bld-brian-farm | 1972 (park practice; Warren map of 1868-69) | stated | The study says the park has consistently used the Warren map and will continue to, partly because the Brady photograph agrees with it in the area around the house, since nothing except the Brady photograph settles the differences between the Warren and Cope maps | #s3
23 | fences | Brady photograph clearly shows a post and plank fence at the northeast corner of the Brien house where the Warren map shows a post and rail | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-HOUSE1 | 1863 (photograph shortly after the battle); the Warren map of 1868-69 differs | conflict | The study says the Warren map shows a post and rail fence in the place where the Brady photograph clearly shows a post and plank fence | #s4
23 | fences | The planks of that fence do not appear to be painted | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm | 1863 (photograph shortly after the battle) | stated | The study says that in the Brady photograph the planks of the post and plank fence at the northeast corner of the house do not appear to be painted | planks do not appear to be painted. | #s4
23 | general | The 1972 fencing plan rests on the Brady photograph, the 1869 Warren map and the planners' own judgments | bld-brian-farm,FEN-brian | 1972 (planning basis) | stated | The study says its recommendations for a fencing and farmyard layout plan are based on the Brady photograph, the 1869 Warren map and judgments that seem logical from their information | =Recommendations for a current fencing and farmyard layout plan are based on the Brady photograph, the {{186?|1869}} Warren map, and judgments that seem logical based on their information.
24 | fences | A stone wall slightly east of the Brien barn is confirmed by the Warren map and Tilberg's research on field defense work | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-BARN1 | ~1868-1869 (Warren map) and the park historian's research on field defenses | stated | The study says the Warren map and Dr. Tilberg's research on field defense work confirm the presence of a stone wall slightly to the east of the barn | =The Warren map and Dr. Tilberg's research on field defense work confirm the presence of this stone wall slightly to the east of the barn.
25 | buildings | Bake oven of the Brien house, documented in shape and location by the Brady photograph; the outhouse is not in the photograph and was never located | bld-brian-farm,STR-BRYAN-HOUSE1 | 1863 (photograph shortly after the battle) | stated | The study says the bake oven is documented in shape and location by the Brady photograph, while an outhouse does not appear in the photograph and has never been located historically | =These are the bake oven, documented in shape and location by the Brady photograph, and an outhouse. The outhouse {{doe3|does}} not appear in the photograph and has never been located historically.
25 | earthworks | Brady photograph shows a stone and rider fence or defense works north and west of the Brien house along the edge of today's Hancock Avenue | FEN-brian,bld-brian-farm,E-17 | 1863 (photograph shortly after the battle; works built quickly at the edge of the tree line according to the park historian's report of 1943) | stated | The study says the Brady photograph indicates a fence or defense works to the north and west of the Brien house along the edge of what is now Hancock Avenue, that a blow-up shows a stone and rider fence, and that Dr. Tilberg's 1943 report on field defenses describes the defense works built quickly at the edge of the tree line | =The Brady photograph does indicate the presence of a fence or defense works to the north and west of the Brien House, along the edge of what is now Hancock Avenue. A blow-up of this portion of the photograph indicates a stone and rider fence. Dr. Tilberg's {{I9I43|1943}} report on Field Defenses on the Battlefield, clearly describes the defense works built quickly at the edge of the tree line, and so accounts for the fencing material evident in the background.
